    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, i'm working on a loft addition for a client in oregon. we're using douglas fir for the main support. i need to figure out the span for a heavy beam to hold up the new floor, but i'm worried about deflection and if there are any specific load distribution rules i should be looking at for timber framing. what's the best way to calculate this to make sure the floor doesn't feel bouncy or sag over time?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "For calculating beam spans in Douglas Fir, you must consult the applicable building codes, such as the International Residential Code (IRC) or local Oregon amendments, as these dictate load capacities and deflection limits. You'll need to determine the tributary area each beam supports to find the design loads (dead and live). Then, use beam design formulas or software that accounts for the specific lumber grade and allows you to check both strength (bending stress) and serviceability (deflection). Keeping the calculated deflection within code limits is key to preventing that bouncy feeling.",
      "source": "target",
