    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. but sometimes even with graded wood, you get these weird issues where the structure just feels \"off\" or there's a sudden failure in a spot that looked totally fine on the outside. besides just the regular knots or rot, are there any other random things that could cause a load-bearing beam to fail unexpectedly or act unpredictable?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Beyond rot and large knots, internal checks for unpredictable behavior should include checking for concealed bearing stresses, especially near connections, and checking for warping or twisting due to uneven moisture absorption. Hidden internal stresses can also arise from improper connections—such as insufficient bolt bearing or poorly distributed load transfer at splice points. Unexpected failure can sometimes be rooted in differential movement between materials or inadequate support conditions.",
      "source": "target",
